{"kind": "report", "step": 13, "ts": "2026-11-18T03:41:55.305929+00:00", "data": {"category": "dns", "host": "dns1.lan", "component": "dns_rate_limit", "cause": "The primary DNS on dns1.lan rate-limits the whole 10.20.0.0/24 to only 20 q/s (ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4: 24), so ordinary traffic bursts make it silently drop queries; a client that times out retries against its second server gw.lan, whose unbound has DNS-rebinding protection (private-address: 10.0.0.0/8) that strips the private 10.20.0.50 answer for *.lan, returning an empty NOERROR that getaddrinfo reports as ENOTFOUND until the burst passes.", "evidence": ["dig @10.20.0.3 nas.lan returns 'nas.lan. 10 IN A 10.20.0.50' (authoritative rewrite works): run 'dig @10.20.0.3 nas.lan'", "dig @10.20.0.1 nas.lan returns status NOERROR but ANSWER: 0 \u2014 no address at all: run 'dig @10.20.0.1 nas.lan'", "/etc/resolv.conf on mon.lan lists nameserver 10.20.0.3 then 10.20.0.1, so a slow/dropped first-server query falls through to gw.lan: run 'cat /etc/resolv.conf'", "dns1 config sets ratelimit: 20 with ratelimit_subnet_len_ipv4: 24, i.e. 20 q/s for the entire /24: run 'cat /opt/primary-dns/config.yaml'", "journalctl shows exactly a few per-day bursts where the whole subnet exceeds that limit and queries are dropped: 'ratelimit: 10.20.0.0/24 exceeded 20 q/s, dropping 23/31/19 queries' at 02:14, 09:41 and 13:02 on Mar 03, each immediately followed by 'back under limit': run 'journalctl -u primary-dns -n 40'", "gw.lan unbound.conf comments 'DNS rebinding protection: drop answers for public names that point at private addresses' with 'private-address: 10.0.0.0/8', while forwarding lan. to 10.20.0.3 \u2014 so the fallback path yields an empty answer for .lan names: run 'cat /var/unbound/unbound.conf'"], "action": "change_config", "recommendation": "Raise/remove the rate limit on dns1's primary-dns (e.g. increase ratelimit far above normal peak, or exempt your own LAN/client traffic) so legitimate bursts aren't dropped. Also exempt the local *.lan rewrite answers from gw.lan's private-address/harden filtering so a query that falls through to gw.lan still returns 10.20.0.50 instead of an empty answer. Internet resolution is unaffected because those answers use public addresses and forward over DoT; nothing needs rebooting or hardware work.", "detail": "Aggressive ratelimit on primary-dns drops LAN bursts", "contributing": {"host": "gw.lan", "component": "dns_resolver_config"}}}
